178 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 100 0 R/T(Village Vistas: Our Village Plum Trees)/E(Village Vistas: Our Village Plum Trees)/Pg 11 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

179 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 100 0 R/E(To walk down our streets in February and March is to see a quiet change of color. Long before the official start of spring, and well before the famous Cherry trees begin their pale-pink show, the Purple Leaf Plums have already taken over the neighborhood. They are the village\220s early birds, bringing a sudden splash of color to the sidewalk while the rest of the trees are still waking up from winter.)/Pg 11 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

180 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 100 0 R/E(These trees are often part of a friendly mix-up, as many people mistake them for true Japanese Cherries. But the careful neighbor knows how to tell them apart. While a cherry tree usually waits for its blossoms to fall before its green leaves appear, the Purple Leaf Plum prefers to do both at once. It grows deep, wine-colored leaves that act as a dark backdrop to its delicate, light-pink flowers.)/Pg 11 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

181 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 100 0 R/E(There is a bit of local history in these branches, too. The "Krauter Vesuvius" variety, which we see all over Sunnyvale, was actually a California invention. It was bred in 1957 to handle the specific, dry heat of our local summers. However, their family tree goes back much further. Almost every purple plum in the West can be traced back to a single tree found in 1880 by Monsieur Pissard, the gardener to the Shah of Persia. He brought the plant to France, and from there, it eventually made its way to the gardens of the New World.)/Pg 11 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

182 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 100 0 R/E(While they are beautiful to look at, they are also useful to the village\220s smallest residents. Since they bloom so early, they are a vital food source for local bees waking up from the cold. And while the small, tart fruits that appear in late summer are safe to eat, most are left for the birds and squirrels who live in the heights of neighborhood parks.)/Pg 11 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

183 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 100 0 R/E(As we enjoy this early gift of color, it is a lovely reminder that in our corner of the valley, spring doesn\220t wait for the calendar; it simply waits for the first plum tree to bloom.)/Pg 11 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

184 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 100 0 R/E(Submit Your Sightings!)/Pg 11 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

185 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 100 0 R/E(Have you spotted a unique bird in your backyard or a beautiful bloom on your block? We want to feature your photos! Please send your images to editor@lvnasv.org by the 15th of the month to be included in our next issue. Let\220s explore the village together!)/Pg 11 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

